# Ansible Installation and First Commands

> For the full guide, see [Ansible_installation.md](Ansible_installation.md) and [docs/GETTING_STARTED.md](docs/GETTING_STARTED.md).

## Install Ansible on a control node

Use a supported method from the [official documentation](https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/installation_guide/index.html). Example using a virtual environment:

```bash
python3 --version
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install ansible ansible-lint
ansible --version
```

## Configure inventory and connectivity

```bash
cp inventory/hosts.ini.example inventory/hosts.ini
# Edit inventory/hosts.ini before running commands.
ansible-inventory -i inventory/hosts.ini --graph
ansible all -i inventory/hosts.ini -m ansible.builtin.ping
```

## Create a directory on target hosts

Prefer the file module to an ad-hoc shell command because it declares the desired state and can be safely re-run:

```bash
ansible all -i inventory/hosts.ini -m ansible.builtin.file \
  -a "path=~/devops state=directory mode=0750"
```

## Run a playbook

```bash
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.ini playbooks/hello.yml --syntax-check
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.ini playbooks/hello.yml --check --diff
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.ini playbooks/hello.yml
```

Check mode is best-effort; review the playbook and output before making changes. Only target systems you are authorized to administer.
